Experiment 5: Connect the Computers in Local Area Network
Objective: To build a Local Area Network (LAN) with two computers and a switch, assign IP addresses, and verify connectivity.

Phase 1: The Setup (Opening the Software)
Launch Cisco Packet Tracer.

You will be greeted with a blank white workspace. Look at the bottom left corner of the screen. You will see a box with several icons (Routers, Switches, End Devices, Connections, etc.). This is your Device Palette.

Look at the top left corner. You will see a row of icons. The one that looks like a black arrow (Select Tool) is currently active. This is your mouse.

Phase 2: Adding the Devices
Add the Switch:

In the bottom left palette, click on the Network Devices icon (it looks like a green router/switch).

In the sub-menu that appears, click on Switches.

You will see a list of switches. Click and hold the 2960 switch, and drag it into the middle of the white workspace.

Add the PCs:

In the bottom left palette, click on the End Devices icon (it looks like a computer monitor).

Click and hold the PC-PT device, and drag it to the left side of the switch.

Click and hold another PC-PT, and drag it to the right side of the switch.

You now have PC0, PC1, and Switch0 on your screen.

Phase 3: Connecting the Devices (The Cables)
Rule: Connecting a PC to a Switch requires a Straight-Through cable.

In the bottom left palette, click the Connections icon (the orange lightning bolt).

You will see different cable types. Click the Copper Straight-Through cable (the solid black line).

Connect PC0:

Click on PC0. A menu will pop up showing its ports. Click FastEthernet0.

Now, click on the Switch. A menu will pop up. Click FastEthernet0/1.

Connect PC1:

The Straight-Through cable should still be selected. If not, click the lightning bolt and select it again.

Click on PC1. Select FastEthernet0.

Click on the Switch. Select FastEthernet0/2.

The "Waiting Game": Look at the cables. You will see little red triangles on the ends. Wait 10-15 seconds. The switch is running a protocol (Spanning Tree) to make sure there are no loops. The red triangles will turn green. (If they stay red, the cable is loose or the switch hasn't finished booting).

Phase 4: Configuring IP Addresses (PC0)
Rule: For the PCs to talk, they must be on the same network (e.g., 192.168.1.x) and have the same Subnet Mask.

Click on PC0. A window will pop up.

Click on the Desktop tab at the top of the window.

Click on the IP Configuration button.

You will see a form. Fill it out exactly like this:

IP Address: 192.168.1.1 (The first PC gets .1)

Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0 (Click inside this box, and it will auto-fill).

Default Gateway: Leave this blank. (A gateway is only needed when routing to another network, which we will do in Exp 6).

Close the PC0 window.

Phase 5: Configuring IP Addresses (PC1)
Click on PC1.

Go to the Desktop tab > IP Configuration.

Fill it out exactly like this:

IP Address: 192.168.1.2 (The second PC gets .2)

Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0

Default Gateway: Leave blank.

Close the PC1 window.

Phase 6: The Verification (The Ping Test)
Rule: ping is a network utility used to test if a device can reach another device. It sends an ICMP Echo Request and waits for an Echo Reply.

Click on PC0.

Go to the Desktop tab.

Click on the Command Prompt icon.

A black window will appear with C:\>.

Type this exactly and press Enter: ping 192.168.1.2

What to expect:

The first line might say Request timed out. This is normal. PC0 is asking "Who has 192.168.1.2?", and it takes a split second for the switch to find PC1 (ARP).

The next 3 lines should say Reply from 192.168.1.2: bytes=32 time=1ms TTL=128.

You should see a Ping statistics summary: Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 4, Lost = 0 (0% loss).

Do not close this window. Leave it open.

Phase 7: Making the File Look Professional (For Grading)
Look at the top toolbar. Find the Place Note icon (it looks like a little yellow sticky note).

Click it, and then click on the white workspace directly under PC0.

Type:
PC0
IP: 192.168.1.1
Mask: 255.255.255.0

Click the Select Tool (the black arrow at the top left) to stop typing.

Repeat the process and place a note under PC1:
PC1
IP: 192.168.1.2
Mask: 255.255.255.0

Optional: Double-click the notes to change the text color (blue for IPs looks great).

Save the File: Go to File > Save As.

Name it Exp_05_LAN.pkt.

Click Save.

💡 Troubleshooting (If things go wrong)
The triangles are red: Click the "Fast Forward Time" button (the clock with a play button at the bottom left of the screen). If they stay red, delete the cable (click the red X icon, then click the cable) and re-connect it.

Ping says "Destination host unreachable": Your IP addresses are wrong. Check that PC0 is 192.168.1.1 and PC1 is 192.168.1.2. They must match the Subnet Mask 255.255.255.0.

Ping says "Request timed out" for all 4 packets: You might have connected the PCs using a Crossover cable instead of a Straight-Through cable. Delete the cables and try again with the solid black line.

Let me know when you have successfully built this and seen the green triangles and successful ping! Once you confirm, I will write out this exact same, highly-detailed, beginner-proof guide for Experiment 6 (Router Configuration).

