Here is the ultimate, zero-to-hero guide for Experiment 6.

This guide assumes you are starting with a completely blank Packet Tracer file.

Experiment 6: Router Configuration using Packet Tracer
Objective: To connect two different networks using a router, configure the router's interfaces via the CLI (Command Line Interface), and verify end-to-end connectivity.

Phase 1: Adding the Devices
Open a brand new blank file in Packet Tracer (File > New).

Add the Router:

Bottom left palette > Network Devices > Routers.

Drag the 1941 router to the middle of the workspace.

Add the Switches:

Bottom left palette > Network Devices > Switches.

Drag two 2960 switches into the workspace. Place one on the left, one on the right.

Add the PCs:

Bottom left palette > End Devices.

Drag two PC-PT devices. Place one far left, one far right.

Phase 2: Cabling the Network
Rule: Use Copper Straight-Through cables to connect different devices (PC to Switch, Switch to Router).

Click the Connections icon (orange lightning bolt) > select Copper Straight-Through (solid black line).

Left Side Connections:

Click PC0 > select FastEthernet0 > click the Left Switch > select FastEthernet0/1.

Click the Left Switch > select FastEthernet0/2 > click the Router > select GigabitEthernet0/0. (Note: 1941 routers use Gigabit ports, not FastEthernet).

Right Side Connections:

Click PC1 > select FastEthernet0 > click the Right Switch > select FastEthernet0/1.

Click the Right Switch > select FastEthernet0/2 > click the Router > select GigabitEthernet0/1.

The Waiting Game: You will see red triangles on the cables connecting to the router. This is normal. Router interfaces are shut down by default. We will turn them on in Phase 4.

Phase 3: Configuring the PCs (IP Addressing)
Rule: The PCs need an IP address, Subnet Mask, and a Default Gateway. The Gateway is the router's IP address, which acts as the "door" to the other network.

Configure PC0:

Click PC0 > Desktop tab > IP Configuration.

IP Address: 192.168.1.1

Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0 (Press Tab to auto-fill).

Default Gateway: 192.168.1.2 (This is the router's Left IP, which we will set next).

Close the window.

Configure PC1:

Click PC1 > Desktop tab > IP Configuration.

IP Address: 192.168.2.1

Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0

Default Gateway: 192.168.2.2 (This is the router's Right IP).

Close the window.

Phase 4: Configuring the Router (The CLI)
Rule: You must assign an IP address to the router interfaces and issue the no shutdown command to turn them on.

Click on the Router.

Go to the CLI tab.

If it asks "Would you like to enter the initial configuration dialog?", type no and press Enter.

Press Enter again to get to the Router> prompt.

Type the following commands exactly. Press Enter after every single line.

text
enable
configure terminal
interface GigabitEthernet0/0
ip address 192.168.1.2 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface GigabitEthernet0/1
ip address 192.168.2.2 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
Watch the lights: As soon as you type the first no shutdown, you should see a message say %LINK-5-CHANGED: Interface GigabitEthernet0/0, changed state to up. Look at your topology; the red triangles on the left side should now be green. After the second no shutdown, the right side will turn green.

Phase 5: The Verification (The Ping Test)
Rule: We are testing if PC0 (on Network 1) can reach PC1 (on Network 2) through the router.

Click PC0 > Desktop > Command Prompt.

Type ping 192.168.2.1 and press Enter.

What to expect:

The first packet will likely say Request timed out. (The router needs to use ARP to find PC1's MAC address).

The next three packets should say Reply from 192.168.2.1...

You should see Packets: Sent = 4, Received = 3, Lost = 1 (25% loss).

Leave this Command Prompt window open.

Phase 6: Making the File Look Professional
Click the Place Note icon (sticky note at the top).

Click under PC0 and type: PC0: 192.168.1.1 (GW: 192.168.1.2)

Click the black arrow to stop typing.

Place another note under PC1: PC1: 192.168.2.1 (GW: 192.168.2.2)

Place a note under the Router: Router: Gig0/0 = 192.168.1.2 | Gig0/1 = 192.168.2.2

Save the file: File > Save As > Name it Exp_06_Router_Config.pkt.

💡 Troubleshooting (If things go wrong)
The triangles stay red after no shutdown: Ensure you typed the interface name correctly. It is GigabitEthernet0/0, not FastEthernet0/0.

Ping says "Destination host unreachable": You forgot to put the Default Gateway in the PC configuration. Go back to Phase 3 and make sure 192.168.1.2 is in PC0's Gateway and 192.168.2.2 is in PC1's Gateway.

Ping completely fails: Check your cables. Did you connect the Left Switch to Gig0/0 and the Right Switch to Gig0/1? If you mixed them up, the IP addresses won't match the network.

