Here is the ultimate, zero-to-hero guide for Experiment 8.

This is the most visually impressive lab in the manual. It simulates a real corporate network (with multiple PCs) accessing the public internet (simulated by servers) through a single public IP address using a router.

Experiment 8: Examine Network Address Translation (NAT)
Objective: To configure NAT Overload (PAT) on a router, allowing multiple private IP addresses on a LAN to share a single public IP address to reach the internet.

Phase 1: Adding the Devices
Open a brand new blank file in Packet Tracer.

Add the Router:

Network Devices > Routers > Drag 1 (1941) router to the middle.

Add the Switches:

Network Devices > Switches > Drag 2 (2960) switches (one left, one right).

Add the End Devices:

End Devices > Drag 3 PCs and 1 Laptop to the left side.

End Devices > Drag 2 Servers (Server-PT) to the right side.

Phase 2: Cabling the Network
Rule: Use Copper Straight-Through cables for all connections in this lab.

Click the Connections icon (lightning bolt) > Copper Straight-Through.

Left Side (Corporate LAN):

Connect the 3 PCs and the Laptop to the Left Switch (use ports Fa0/1, Fa0/2, Fa0/3, Fa0/4).

Connect the Left Switch (GigabitEthernet0/1) to the Router (GigabitEthernet0/0).

Right Side (Internet/Data Center):

Connect Server0 to the Right Switch (Fa0/1).

Connect Server1 to the Right Switch (Fa0/2).

Connect the Right Switch (GigabitEthernet0/1) to the Router (GigabitEthernet0/1).

The router cables will have red triangles until we configure the router.

Phase 3: Configuring the Private Devices (Inside Network)
Rule: All internal devices must be on the same network (192.168.1.x) and use the router's inside IP as their Gateway.

PC0: Desktop > IP Configuration > IP: 192.168.1.10 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 192.168.1.1

PC1: Desktop > IP Configuration > IP: 192.168.1.11 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 192.168.1.1

PC2: Desktop > IP Configuration > IP: 192.168.1.12 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 192.168.1.1

Laptop0: (Go to Config tab > FastEthernet0 on the left. If it says "Port Status: Off", click it to turn it ON. Then go to Desktop > IP Config). IP: 192.168.1.13 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 192.168.1.1

Phase 4: Configuring the Public Devices (Outside Network)
Rule: The servers simulating the internet must have public IPs (e.g., 200.1.1.x) and use the router's outside IP as their Gateway.

Server0: Desktop > IP Configuration > IP: 200.1.1.10 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 200.1.1.1

Server1: Desktop > IP Configuration > IP: 200.1.1.11 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 200.1.1.1

Phase 5: Basic Router Setup
Click the Router > CLI tab. (Type no if asked).

Type these commands to assign IP addresses and turn the ports on:

text
enable
configure terminal
interface GigabitEthernet0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface GigabitEthernet0/1
ip address 200.1.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
(Wait for the red triangles to turn green).

Phase 6: The "Before NAT" Test (Crucial for your report)
Click PC0 > Desktop > Command Prompt.

Type ping 200.1.1.10 and press Enter.

It will fail (Request timed out).

Why? Because Server0 is on the public internet. When it tries to reply to PC0 (192.168.1.10), the public network doesn't know where that private IP is. It drops the packet. This proves we need NAT. Close the command prompt.

Phase 7: Configuring NAT (The Magic Step)
Go back to the Router CLI.

Type the following commands exactly, pressing Enter after each line:

text
configure terminal
interface GigabitEthernet0/0
ip nat inside
exit
interface GigabitEthernet0/1
ip nat outside
exit
access-list 1 permit 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255
ip nat inside source list 1 interface GigabitEthernet0/1 overload
exit
Phase 8: The "After NAT" Test
Go back to PC0 > Command Prompt.

Type ping 200.1.1.10 and press Enter.

What to expect: The first packet may time out (normal ARP). The next 3 should say Reply from 200.1.1.10... This means NAT is working!

Phase 9: The Proof (Viewing the NAT Table)
Go to the Router CLI.

Type show ip nat translations and press Enter.

What you will see: A table showing that 192.168.1.10 (your private PC) is being translated to 200.1.1.1 (the router's public IP) using different port numbers.

Leave this CLI window open.

Phase 10: Make the File Look Incredible (For Grading)
Add Notes: Use the Place Note tool (sticky note at the top) to label the devices.

Under PC0: PC0: 192.168.1.10 (GW: .1)

Under Server0: Server0: 200.1.1.10 (GW: .1)

Under the Router: NAT Router: Inside=192.168.1.1 | Outside=200.1.1.1

Leave Windows Open: Make sure the Router CLI (showing the show ip nat translations table) and PC0's Command Prompt (showing the successful ping) are both open on your screen.

Save the file: File > Save As > Name it Exp_08_NAT.pkt.

💡 Troubleshooting
Ping fails even after NAT: Make sure you typed ip nat inside on Gig0/0 and ip nat outside on Gig0/1. If you mix them up, NAT will break.

Access List error: Make sure you type the wildcard mask 0.0.0.255 (with the dots) correctly. It is not a regular subnet mask.

The show ip nat translations table is empty: You probably waited too long after pinging. The table clears after a few minutes. Run a new ping from PC0 and immediately check the table again.

