Here is the monumental, zero-to-hero guide for Experiment 9.

This is the biggest lab in the manual. You are building a "Triangle" of 3 routers, 3 switches, and multiple PCs. You will configure OSPF (Open Shortest Path First) so that all the routers automatically share their routes and every PC can talk to every other PC.

Experiment 9: Simulate the Implementation of Various Routing Protocols (OSPF)
Objective: To configure a multi-router network using OSPF so that routers dynamically learn routes and PCs in different LANs can communicate.

Phase 1: Adding the Devices
Open a brand new blank file in Packet Tracer.

Add 3 Routers: Network Devices > Routers > Drag 3 (1941) routers. Arrange them in a triangle (Top Left, Top Right, Bottom Middle). We will call them R1, R2, and R3.

Add 3 Switches: Network Devices > Switches > Drag 3 (2960) switches. Place one next to each router.

Add 3 PCs: End Devices > Drag 3 PCs. Place one next to each switch.

Phase 2: The "Extra Port" Hack (CRITICAL STEP)
Rule: The 1941 router only has Gig0/0 and Gig0/1. To connect it to two other routers and a switch, we need a third port (Gig0/2).

Click on Router 1.

Go to the Physical tab.

Click the green Power Switch on the router image (it will turn off).

On the left side, under "Modules", find GLC-T. Click and drag it into the empty black slot on the right side of the router.

Click the Power Switch again to turn it back on.

Repeat this for Router 2 and Router 3. (You now have Gig0/0, Gig0/1, and Gig0/2 on all routers).

Phase 3: Cabling the Network
LAN Cables (Copper Straight-Through - solid black line):

PC0 (Fa0) --- Switch0 (Fa0/1)

PC1 (Fa0) --- Switch1 (Fa0/1)

PC2 (Fa0) --- Switch2 (Fa0/1)

Switch0 (Gig0/1) --- R1 (Gig0/0)

Switch1 (Gig0/1) --- R2 (Gig0/0)

Switch2 (Gig0/1) --- R3 (Gig0/0)

Router Cables (Copper CROSSOVER - dashed black line):

R1 (Gig0/1) --- R2 (Gig0/1)

R2 (Gig0/2) --- R3 (Gig0/2)

R3 (Gig0/1) --- R1 (Gig0/2)

Phase 4: IP Addressing Scheme
Rule: We use 192.168.x.0/24 for LANs and 10.x.x.0/24 for router-to-router links.
Configure the PCs:

PC0: IP: 192.168.1.10 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | GW: 192.168.1.1

PC1: IP: 192.168.2.10 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | GW: 192.168.2.1

PC2: IP: 192.168.3.10 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | GW: 192.168.3.1

Phase 5: Basic Router Configuration
Go to the CLI of each router and configure the interfaces.

R1 (Top Left):

text
enable
configure terminal
interface Gig0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/1
ip address 10.1.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/2
ip address 10.3.3.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
R2 (Top Right):

text
enable
configure terminal
interface Gig0/0
ip address 192.168.2.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/1
ip address 10.1.1.2 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/2
ip address 10.2.2.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
R3 (Bottom Middle):

text
enable
configure terminal
interface Gig0/0
ip address 192.168.3.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/1
ip address 10.3.3.2 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/2
ip address 10.2.2.2 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
(Wait for all red triangles to turn green).

Phase 6: The "Before OSPF" Test
Go to PC0 > Desktop > Command Prompt.

Type ping 192.168.2.10 (PC1).

It will fail. Why? R1 knows about LAN 1, but it doesn't know how to reach LAN 2 or LAN 3. We need OSPF to teach it.

Phase 7: Configuring OSPF (The Magic Step)
Rule: OSPF requires you to announce all the directly connected networks using the network command. The wildcard mask 0.0.0.255 tells the router to look at the first 3 numbers.

On R1:

text
configure terminal
router ospf 1
network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.3.3.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
exit
On R2:

text
configure terminal
router ospf 1
network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.2.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
exit
On R3:

text
configure terminal
router ospf 1
network 192.168.3.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.3.3.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.2.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
exit
Phase 8: The "After OSPF" Test
Wait about 30 seconds. OSPF needs time to exchange "Hello" packets and build its routing tables.

Go to PC0 > Desktop > Command Prompt.

Type ping 192.168.2.10 (PC1). It should succeed now!

Type ping 192.168.3.10 (PC2). It should succeed too!

Leave this Command Prompt window open.

Phase 9: Make the File Look Professional
Add Notes:

Under PC0: LAN 1: 192.168.1.0/24

Under PC1: LAN 2: 192.168.2.0/24

Under PC2: LAN 3: 192.168.3.0/24

Verify Routing Table (For your report): Go to the R1 CLI and type show ip route. You should see a list of routes marked with an O (which stands for OSPF), proving the router learned the other networks dynamically.

Save the file: File > Save As > Name it Exp_09_Routing_Protocols.pkt.

💡 Troubleshooting
I don't have a Gig0/2 port: You skipped the Phase 2 "Extra Port" hack. Turn the router off, drag the GLC-T module in, and turn it back on.

Ping still fails after OSPF: Ensure the network commands match your IP addresses exactly (e.g., 10.1.1.0 not 10.1.1.1).

The show ip route command doesn't show O routes: OSPF might still be converging. Wait another 30 seconds, or type clear ip ospf process and press Enter, then type yes.

why this exists
This is a fantastic screenshot. You are seeing the red triangles on the dashed black line connecting your two routers.

You are likely wondering: "I connected the cable, why is it not green like the others?"

Here is exactly why this happens, and how to fix it:

1. You are missing the "No Shutdown" Command
In Packet Tracer (and real Cisco routers), all router interfaces are administratively shut down (turned off) by default for security. Even if the cable is physically plugged in perfectly, the router will not send any data out of it until you explicitly turn it on.

The fix: You must go into the CLI of Router0 and Router1 and type no shutdown for the interfaces connected to that dashed cable. (If you look at my Phase 5 guide, those commands are there).

2. The Crossover Cable is Correct!
Notice how I specified a Copper Cross-Over cable (the dashed black line) for router-to-router connections? You used the correct cable! (Connecting two routers directly requires a crossover cable because they are "similar" devices).

Side note: In modern Packet Tracer, Cisco routers actually have "Auto-MDIX" technology, which means a straight-through cable would also work. But using the dashed crossover cable is the technically correct way to show your instructor you know your cabling rules.

3. Don't Panic if it Stays Red for a Moment
Sometimes, even after you type no shutdown, the triangle might stay red for a few seconds while the routers negotiate the link speed and duplex. Just wait 10-15 seconds.

How to Fix it Right Now:
Click on Router0.

Go to the CLI tab.

Type:

text
enable
configure terminal
interface GigabitEthernet0/1
no shutdown
(Assuming Gig0/1 is the port you plugged the dashed cable into).

Do the exact same thing on Router1.

Once both routers have their ports turned on, the red triangles will turn green.

(Keep going with the OSPF commands in Phase 7! Once OSPF is configured, you will be able to ping across those routers).

all green still not wroking
I see exactly what you have built. You have built a linear topology (PC0 -> Switch0 -> Router0 -> dashed cable -> Router1 -> Switch1 -> PC1).

You are completely right: All the lights are green, which means the physical cables and no shutdown commands worked perfectly.

However, there is a major difference between a physical connection (green light) and a logical route (data actually flowing). Here is why it's failing and how to fix it.

Why is it failing?
Since you have two routers in the middle, Router0 only knows about the Left side (192.168.1.x), and Router1 only knows about the Right side (192.168.2.x).
When PC0 sends a ping to PC1, the packet reaches Router0. Router0 says, "I don't know where PC1 is, and I don't have a route to Router1's network," and it drops the packet.

You need to either use a Routing Protocol (like OSPF) or a Static Route to tell the routers how to find each other.

Before we do that, we must verify your IP addresses are correct. Because you built this as a straight line (not a triangle), the IP scheme changes slightly from the 3-router lab.

Phase 1: Verify your IP Addresses
Since you have a dashed line in the middle, you need a "transit network" between the routers.
Here is exactly what your IP configuration should be:

PC0: 192.168.1.10 | GW: 192.168.1.1

Router0 (Gig0/0 - Left): 192.168.1.1 | Mask: 255.255.255.0

Router0 (Gig0/1 - Right): 10.1.1.1 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 (This is the dashed link)

Router1 (Gig0/0 - Left): 10.1.1.2 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 (This is the dashed link)

Router1 (Gig0/1 - Right): 192.168.2.1 | Mask: 255.255.255.0

PC1: 192.168.2.10 | GW: 192.168.2.1

(If your routers don't have the 10.1.1.x addresses on the dashed link, that is the first thing you need to fix using the CLI interface Gig0/1 and ip address commands).

Phase 2: Add OSPF (The Fix)
We will use OSPF to tell the routers about each other's networks.

Go to Router0 CLI and type:

text
enable
configure terminal
router ospf 1
network 192.168.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
exit
Go to Router1 CLI and type:

text
enable
configure terminal
router ospf 1
network 192.168.2.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
network 10.1.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0
exit
Phase 3: The Test
Go to PC0 > Desktop > Command Prompt.

Type ping 192.168.2.10 (Make sure this matches PC1's actual IP).

The first ping might fail (Request timed out) because OSPF takes about 10-30 seconds to exchange routing tables.

Wait 30 seconds, then try the ping again. It should succeed!

If it still fails after 30 seconds, tell me exactly what the ping error says (Destination host unreachable or Request timed out).

request timed out
"Request timed out" is actually a good sign—it means the packet made it out of PC0, but it got lost in the middle.

Because you have a linear chain (PC0 -> Switch0 -> R0 -> R1 -> Switch1 -> PC1), we have to be very careful with the IP addresses on the dashed link. If Router0 and Router1 aren't in the same subnet on that dashed cable, they won't be able to pass the traffic.

Let's run a quick diagnostic to find the exact break.

Step 1: Check the Router CLI (Router0)
Click on Router0.

Go to the CLI tab.

Type enable (press Enter), then type show ip interface brief (press Enter).

Look at the screen. For GigabitEthernet0/1 (the port connected to the dashed cable), look at the IP Address and the Status.

It should have an IP like 10.1.1.1 or 192.168.2.1 and say up / up.

Now type ping 10.1.1.2 (or whatever IP you gave to Router1's dashed port).

Did it reply? If not, the dashed cable's IP addresses are wrong.

Step 2: Check the PC1 Gateway
Click on PC1.

Go to Desktop > IP Configuration.

Look at the Default Gateway.

Does it match Router1's LAN IP address?

If Router1's Gig0/1 (LAN) is 192.168.2.1, then PC1's Gateway must be 192.168.2.1. If it is blank or says 192.168.1.1, the reply will never get back to PC1.

Step 3: The Routing Table Check (The most common error)
Go to Router0 > CLI.

Type show ip route.

You are looking for a line that says O 192.168.2.0/24 [110/2] via 10.1.1.2....

If it is missing: The OSPF commands didn't take, or the network addresses in your OSPF network commands don't match the actual interface IP addresses.

The Fastest Fix (If you just want it to work right now):
If the dashed link IP addresses are confusing you, we can use Static Routes instead. It's simpler.

On Router0 CLI:

text
enable
configure terminal
ip route 192.168.2.0 255.255.255.0 10.1.1.2
(Note: Change 10.1.1.2 to whatever IP address you actually assigned to Router1's dashed port).

On Router1 CLI:

text
enable
configure terminal
ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.1.1.1
(Note: Change 10.1.1.1 to whatever IP address you actually assigned to Router0's dashed port).

Once you type those two static routes, go back to PC0 and ping PC1 (192.168.2.10). Let me know if it works!

Ah! I see the problem immediately in your screenshot.

Look at the IP address for GigabitEthernet0/0. It says 192.168.1.10.

That is the IP address of a PC, not a router! In Exp 9, we established a golden rule: Routers get the .1 address (the gateway), and PCs get the .10 address.

If your router is using 192.168.1.10, it is fighting with PC0 for the same IP address. This causes an "IP Conflict," and the network shuts down.

Also, look at GigabitEthernet0/1. It says 10.2.2.2. This is the link to the other router. That means the other router (Router1) must be 10.2.2.1 for them to talk.

Here is exactly how to fix this step-by-step so your ping works.

Step 1: Fix Router0's IP Addresses
Go to the CLI of Router0 (which is what is shown in your screenshot) and type this to fix the interface facing PC0:

text
enable
configure terminal
interface GigabitEthernet0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
exit
(Now the router is .1, and it will stop fighting with PC0).

Step 2: Configure the Static Routes (The "Fast Fix")
Since we know Router0's dashed port is 10.2.2.2, Router1's dashed port must be 10.2.2.1. Let's tell them how to find each other without relying on OSPF.

Still in the Router0 CLI, type this:

text
ip route 192.168.2.0 255.255.255.0 10.2.2.1
(This tells Router0: "To find the 192.168.2.x network, send the traffic out to your neighbor at 10.2.2.1").

Step 3: Configure Router1
Click on Router1.

Go to the CLI.

Type enable > configure terminal.

Ensure Router1's ports are set up correctly:

text
interface GigabitEthernet0/1
ip address 10.2.2.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
(Notice I made this .1 because Router0 is .2).

Now tell Router1 how to find PC0's network:

text
ip route 192.168.1.0 255.255.255.0 10.2.2.2
Step 4: The Final Test
Go to PC0 > Desktop > Command Prompt.

Type ping 192.168.2.10 (Assuming PC1 is .10).

It should work!

(Note: If your PC1 is actually on 192.168.2.x with a gateway of 192.168.2.1, make sure Router1's Gig0/0 interface is set to 192.168.2.1).

Let me know if the ping succeeds now!

