You are moving on to Experiment 10: Connection-Oriented Client-Server Applications with TCP and UDP.

This lab is completely different from the routers. We are going to build a small network with a Server and a Client. You will configure the Server to host a website (HTTP/TCP) and a DNS service (UDP), and then use a PC to access it.

Here is the ultimate, zero-to-hero guide.

Experiment 10: Connection-Oriented Client-Server Applications (TCP/UDP)
Objective: To configure a Server to host HTTP (TCP) and DNS (UDP) services, and verify client access using a Web Browser.

Phase 1: Adding the Devices
Open a brand new blank file in Packet Tracer.

Add the Switch:

Network Devices > Switches > Drag 1 (2960) switch into the middle.

Add the End Devices:

End Devices > Drag 1 PC (Client) to the left side.

End Devices > Drag 1 Server (Server-PT) to the right side.

Phase 2: Cabling the Network
Click the Connections icon (lightning bolt) > Copper Straight-Through (solid black line).

Connect PC0 (FastEthernet0) to Switch0 (FastEthernet0/1).

Connect Server0 (FastEthernet0) to Switch0 (FastEthernet0/2).

(Wait for the green triangles).

Phase 3: Configuring IP Addresses
Rule: Both devices are on the same network, so they don't need a router. We will still fill out the Gateway just for best practice.

PC0 (Client):

Click PC0 > Desktop > IP Configuration.

IP Address: 192.168.1.10

Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0

Default Gateway: 192.168.1.1 (Leave it as the standard gateway).

Server0:

Click Server0 > Desktop > IP Configuration.

IP Address: 192.168.1.100 (Servers usually get static .100 or higher IPs).

Subnet Mask: 255.255.255.0

Default Gateway: 192.168.1.1

Phase 4: Configuring the HTTP Server (TCP)
Rule: HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) uses TCP because it is connection-oriented. It ensures the web page loads completely and without errors.

Click on Server0.

Go to the Services tab at the top.

Click on HTTP in the left menu.

Ensure the HTTP service is ON (the radio button next to "On" should be selected).

Look at the index.html code box. You can scroll down and find the <h1> tags.

Change the text inside to say: <h1>Welcome to Exp 10: The TCP Web Server!</h1>

Click the Save button at the bottom of the code box.

Phase 5: Configuring the DNS Server (UDP)
Rule: DNS (Domain Name System) uses UDP because it is connectionless and fast. It just asks "What is the IP for this name?" and gets a quick reply.

Still on Server0, go to the Services tab.

Click on DNS in the left menu.

Ensure the DNS Service is ON.

In the Name box, type: www.explab.com

In the Address box, type: 192.168.1.100 (The server's own IP).

Click the Add button. You should see it appear in the list below.

Phase 6: The Client Test (HTTP / TCP)
Click on PC0 > Desktop > Web Browser.

In the URL bar at the top, type: http://192.168.1.100 and press Enter.

What to expect: The web page should load instantly, and you will see your custom text: "Welcome to Exp 10: The TCP Web Server!"

Phase 7: The Client Test (DNS / UDP)
Still in the Web Browser on PC0, type: http://www.explab.com and press Enter.

What to expect: The page should load again!

Why this matters: PC0 just used UDP to ask the server for the IP of www.explab.com, and then used TCP to load the page.

Phase 8: Make the File Look Professional
Use the Place Note tool (sticky note at the top) to add labels:

Under PC0: PC0: 192.168.1.10

Under Server0: Server0: 192.168.1.100 (HTTP + DNS)

Leave the Web Browser window open on PC0 showing the successful www.explab.com page.

Go to File > Save As > Name it Exp_10_Client_Server.pkt.

💡 Troubleshooting
The web page fails to load: Check the Server's HTTP service. Make sure it is turned ON and you clicked "Save" after editing the HTML.

The www.explab.com URL fails, but the IP address works: Your DNS is wrong. Go back to Phase 5 and ensure the Name is exactly www.explab.com and the Address is 192.168.1.100.

The browser says "Host Name Unresolved": PC0 cannot reach the DNS server. Check that the cables are green and the IP addresses match.

