Here is the final masterclass guide for Experiment 12 (Wireshark / Packet Analysis).

Since Experiment 11 is pure Python code (which we already covered), Experiment 12 is the perfect way to finish your Packet Tracer journey. We are going to use Packet Tracer's Simulation Mode to act exactly like Wireshark, capturing a packet and analyzing its OSI layers.

Experiment 12: Exploring Tools (Wireshark Simulation)
Objective: To use Packet Tracer's Simulation Mode to capture network traffic, analyze TCP/IP packet headers, and understand the OSI Model in action.

Phase 1: Adding the Devices
Open a brand new blank file.

Add the Router: Network Devices > Routers > Drag 1 (1941) to the middle.

Add the Switch: Network Devices > Switches > Drag 1 (2960) to the left.

Add the PCs: End Devices > Drag 1 PC to the left of the switch.

Add the Server: End Devices > Drag 1 Server (Server-PT) to the right of the router.

Phase 2: Cabling the Network
Click Connections (lightning bolt) > Copper Straight-Through.

PC0 (Fa0) --- Switch0 (Fa0/1)

Switch0 (Gig0/1) --- Router0 (Gig0/0)

Router0 (Gig0/1) --- Server0 (Fa0)

Phase 3: Configuring IP Addresses
Rule: We are simulating a small office (PC0) accessing the internet (Server0).

PC0:

IP: 192.168.1.10 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 192.168.1.1

Server0:

IP: 8.8.8.8 | Mask: 255.255.255.0 | Gateway: 8.8.8.1 (Simulating Google's public DNS)

Router0 (CLI):

text
enable
configure terminal
interface Gig0/0
ip address 192.168.1.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
interface Gig0/1
ip address 8.8.8.1 255.255.255.0
no shutdown
exit
(Wait for all cables to turn green).

Phase 4: Set up the Web Server
Click Server0 > Services tab > HTTP.

Turn HTTP ON.

Edit the HTML to say: Welcome to the Exp 12 Capstone Web Server! and click Save.

Phase 5: The Wireshark Simulation (The Core of Exp 12)
Now we will capture the packet exactly like Wireshark does.

Enter Simulation Mode: Look at the bottom right corner of your Packet Tracer screen. Click the Simulation button (the stopwatch icon).

The screen will turn slightly grey, and an Event List will appear on the right side.

Set the Filter:

On the right panel, click Edit Filters (or the little funnel icon).

Select the IPv4 tab.

Uncheck everything. Then scroll down and check HTTP and TCP.

Close the filter window. (This prevents us from seeing useless background traffic like STP or ARP).

Generate the Traffic:

Click PC0 > Desktop > Web Browser.

Type http://8.8.8.8 and press Enter.

The Magic: Notice that the web page didn't load instantly. The simulation has paused. Look at the Event List on the right; you will see TCP/HTTP packets waiting to move.

Step through the network: At the bottom of the screen, click the Capture/Forward button (the button with the arrow pointing right).

An envelope icon will move from PC0 to the Switch, then to the Router.

Capture the Data: Click on the envelope icon when it is sitting on the Router.

A window will pop up called PDU Information.

Phase 6: Analyzing the Packet (The Wireshark Details)
In the PDU Information window, click the Outbound PDU Details tab. You are now looking at the exact same data Wireshark would show you. Here is what you are looking at (for your lab report):

Layer 2 (Ethernet II): Look at the Source MAC and Destination MAC addresses. This is how the switch moves the frame.

Layer 3 (IPv4): Look at the Source IP (192.168.1.10) and Destination IP (8.8.8.8). This is how the router routes the packet.

Layer 4 (TCP): Look at the Source Port (a random high number like 1025) and the Destination Port (80 for HTTP). This is how the server knows it's a web request.

Phase 7: Make the File Look Professional
Leave the PDU Information window open on your screen (this is your proof).

Use the Place Note tool to add: Exp 12: Packet Capture and OSI Analysis via Simulation Mode.

Go to File > Save As > Name it Exp_12_Wireshark_Simulation.pkt.

(Optional but highly recommended): Take a screenshot of the PDU Details window for your Word document.

💡 Understanding the "Simulation Mode"
If your instructor asks why we use this, you can explain:

"Wireshark captures real packets on a live network. Packet Tracer's Simulation Mode does the exact same thing, but it freezes time so we can inspect the packet headers layer by layer (Layer 2, 3, and 4) to see exactly how the TCP handshake and HTTP requests work."

